---@alias gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2ColumnDataProfileEstimatedNullPercentageEnum "NULL_PERCENTAGE_LEVEL_UNSPECIFIED"|"NULL_PERCENTAGE_VERY_LOW"|"NULL_PERCENTAGE_LOW"|"NULL_PERCENTAGE_MEDIUM"|"NULL_PERCENTAGE_HIGH"

---@alias gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2ColumnDataProfileEstimatedUniquenessScoreEnum "UNIQUENESS_SCORE_LEVEL_UNSPECIFIED"|"UNIQUENESS_SCORE_LOW"|"UNIQUENESS_SCORE_MEDIUM"|"UNIQUENESS_SCORE_HIGH"

---@alias gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2ColumnDataProfileList gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2ColumnDataProfile[]

---@alias gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2ColumnDataProfilePolicyStateEnum "COLUMN_POLICY_STATE_UNSPECIFIED"|"COLUMN_POLICY_TAGGED"

---@alias gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2ColumnDataProfileStateEnum "STATE_UNSPECIFIED"|"RUNNING"|"DONE"

---The field type of `value` and `field` do not need to match to be considered equal, but not all comparisons are possible. EQUAL_TO and NOT_EQUAL_TO attempt to compare even with incompatible types, but all other comparisons are invalid with incompatible types. A `value` of type: - `string` can be compared against all other types - `boolean` can only be compared against other booleans - `integer` can be compared against doubles or a string if the string value can be parsed as an integer. - `double` can be compared against integers or a string if the string can be parsed as a double. - `Timestamp` can be compared against strings in RFC 3339 date string format. - `TimeOfDay` can be compared against timestamps and strings in the format of 'HH:mm:ss'. If we fail to compare do to type mismatch, a warning will be given and the condition will evaluate to false.
---@class gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2Condition
---@field value? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2Value Value to compare against. [Mandatory, except for `EXISTS` tests.]
---@field field? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2FieldId Required. Field within the record this condition is evaluated against.
---@field operator? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2ConditionOperatorEnum|string Required. Operator used to compare the field or infoType to the value.

---@alias gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2ConditionList gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2Condition[]

---@alias gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2ConditionOperatorEnum "RELATIONAL_OPERATOR_UNSPECIFIED"|"EQUAL_TO"|"NOT_EQUAL_TO"|"GREATER_THAN"|"LESS_THAN"|"GREATER_THAN_OR_EQUALS"|"LESS_THAN_OR_EQUALS"|"EXISTS"

---A collection of conditions.
---@class gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2Conditions
---@field conditions? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2ConditionList A collection of conditions.

---A data connection to allow the DLP API to profile data in locations that require additional configuration.
---@class gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2Connection
---@field name? string Output only. Name of the connection: `projects/{project}/locations/{location}/connections/{name}`.
---@field errors? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2ErrorList Output only. Set if status == ERROR, to provide additional details. Will store the last 10 errors sorted with the most recent first.
---@field state? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2ConnectionStateEnum|string Required. The connection's state in its lifecycle.
---@field cloudSql? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2CloudSqlProperties Connect to a Cloud SQL instance.

---@alias gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2ConnectionList gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2Connection[]

---@alias gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2ConnectionStateEnum "CONNECTION_STATE_UNSPECIFIED"|"MISSING_CREDENTIALS"|"AVAILABLE"|"ERROR"

---Represents a container that may contain DLP findings. Examples of a container include a file, table, or database record.
---@class gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2Container
---@field type? string Container type, for example BigQuery or Cloud Storage.
---@field updateTime? string Findings container modification timestamp, if applicable. For Cloud Storage, this field contains the last file modification timestamp. For a BigQuery table, this field contains the last_modified_time property. For Datastore, this field isn't populated.
---@field fullPath? string A string representation of the full container name. Examples: - BigQuery: 'Project:DataSetId.TableId' - Cloud Storage: 'gs://Bucket/folders/filename.txt'
---@field rootPath? string The root of the container. Examples: - For BigQuery table `project_id:dataset_id.table_id`, the root is `dataset_id` - For Cloud Storage file `gs://bucket/folder/filename.txt`, the root is `gs://bucket`
---@field version? string Findings container version, if available ("generation" for Cloud Storage).
---@field relativePath? string The rest of the path after the root. Examples: - For BigQuery table `project_id:dataset_id.table_id`, the relative path is `table_id` - For Cloud Storage file `gs://bucket/folder/filename.txt`, the relative path is `folder/filename.txt`
---@field projectId? string Project where the finding was found. Can be different from the project that owns the finding.

---Type of content to inspect.
---@class gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2ContentItem
---@field conversation? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2Conversation Represents a conversation (either complete or a slice). It is assumed that all included messages are contiguous and ordered in chronological order.
---@field batchContentItem? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2BatchContentItem Represents a batch of items to inspect.
---@field table? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2Table Structured content for inspection. See https://docs.cloud.google.com/sensitive-data-protection/docs/inspecting-text#inspecting_a_table to learn more.
---@field byteItem? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2ByteContentItem Content data to inspect or redact. Replaces `type` and `data`.
---@field value? string String data to inspect or redact.
---@field contentMetadata? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2ContentMetadata User provided metadata for the content.

---Precise location of the finding within a document, record, image, or metadata container.
---@class gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2ContentLocation
---@field batchContentLocation? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2BatchContentLocation Location within a batch of content.
---@field containerVersion? string Finding container version, if available ("generation" for Cloud Storage).
---@field containerTimestamp? string Finding container modification timestamp, if applicable. For Cloud Storage, this field contains the last file modification timestamp. For a BigQuery table, this field contains the last_modified_time property. For Datastore, this field isn't populated.
---@field metadataLocation? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2MetadataLocation Location within the metadata for inspected content.
---@field recordLocation? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2RecordLocation Location within a row or record of a database table.
---@field containerName? string Name of the container where the finding is located. The top level name is the source file name or table name. Names of some common storage containers are formatted as follows: * BigQuery tables: `{project_id}:{dataset_id}.{table_id}` * Cloud Storage files: `gs://{bucket}/{path}` * Datastore namespace: {namespace} Nested names could be absent if the embedded object has no string identifier (for example, an image contained within a document).
---@field documentLocation? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2DocumentLocation Location data for document files.
---@field conversationLocation? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2ConversationLocation Location within a conversation.
---@field imageLocation? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2ImageLocation Location within an image's pixels.

---@alias gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2ContentLocationList gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2ContentLocation[]

---Metadata on content to be scanned.
---@class gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2ContentMetadata
---@field properties? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2KeyValueMetadataPropertyList User provided key-value pairs of content metadata.
---@field fileLabels? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2FileLabelList Optional. The file labels associated with the content.

---A policy to apply to content based on its inspection findings.
---@class gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2ContentPolicy
---@field name? string Output only. Resource name of the policy.
---@field failedToScanSupportedFileType? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2PolicyAction Optional. Action to take if the content is a supported file type and size but fails to be scanned, for example because the file is encrypted or corrupted.
---@field unsupportedFileType? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2PolicyAction Optional. Action to take if the content is an unsupported file type.
---@field inspectConfig? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2InspectConfig Optional. InspectConfig to use to produce findings.
---@field loggingConfigs? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2LoggingConfigList Optional. Log the actions taken by the content policy to external systems.
---@field rules? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2PolicyRuleList Required. Policies to apply, based on the findings returned by inspection. The first rule to match applies.
---@field defaultAction? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2PolicyAction Action to take if the content is scanned and no rules match. Defaults to returning an ALLOW verdict if not set.
---@field createTime? string Output only. The creation timestamp of a contentPolicy; output-only field.
---@field updateTime? string Output only. The last update timestamp of a contentPolicy; output-only field.
---@field errors? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2ErrorList Output only. A stream of errors encountered when the policy was applied. Output only field. Will return the last 100 errors. Whenever the policy is modified this list will be cleared.
---@field inputTooLarge? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2PolicyAction Optional. Action to take if the content is a supported file type but is too large to be scanned.
---@field displayName? string Optional. Display name (max 63 chars)

---@alias gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2ContentPolicyList gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2ContentPolicy[]

---Complete conversation or slice of a conversation. It is assumed that all included messages are contiguous and ordered in chronological order.
---@class gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2Conversation
---@field messages? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2ConversationMessageList Messages exchanged within this conversation. The maximum number of messages allowed is 50k. The order of the messages is assumed to be chronological and will be used to index findings in the response.

---Location within a conversation.
---@class gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2ConversationLocation
---@field messageIndex? number Matches an index of a message in the conversation provided in the request.
---@field allMessages? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2AllMessages If set, indicates that the finding applies to all messages in the conversation.

---Single message in a conversation.
---@class gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2ConversationMessage
---@field messageParts? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2MessagePartList Optional. The parts of the message. Restricted to being at most a single text item. Only one of `content` and `message_parts` can be set.
---@field participantId? string Optional. The identifier of the participant, for example 'test-user' or 'gemini'. The participant ID can contain lowercase letters, numbers, and hyphens; that is, it must match the regular expression: `^[a-z]([a-z0-9-]{0,61}[a-z0-9])?$`. The maximum length is 63 characters.
---@field messageType? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2ConversationMessageMessageTypeEnum|string The type of message.
---@field content? string Deprecated: Use `message_parts` instead. The contents of this message. Only one of `content` and `message_parts` can be set.

---@alias gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2ConversationMessageList gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2ConversationMessage[]

---@alias gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2ConversationMessageMessageTypeEnum "MESSAGE_TYPE_UNSPECIFIED"|"CONTENT"|"CONTEXT"

---Pseudonymization method that generates deterministic encryption for the given input. Outputs a base64 encoded representation of the encrypted output. Uses AES-SIV based on the RFC https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc5297.
---@class gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2CryptoDeterministicConfig
---@field cryptoKey? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2CryptoKey The key used by the encryption function. For deterministic encryption using AES-SIV, the provided key is internally expanded to 64 bytes prior to use.
---@field surrogateInfoType? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2InfoType The custom info type to annotate the surrogate with. This annotation will be applied to the surrogate by prefixing it with the name of the custom info type followed by the number of characters comprising the surrogate. The following scheme defines the format: {info type name}({surrogate character count}):{surrogate} For example, if the name of custom info type is 'MY_TOKEN_INFO_TYPE' and the surrogate is 'abc', the full replacement value will be: 'MY_TOKEN_INFO_TYPE(3):abc' This annotation identifies the surrogate when inspecting content using the custom info type 'Surrogate'. This facilitates reversal of the surrogate when it occurs in free text. Note: For record transformations where the entire cell in a table is being transformed, surrogates are not mandatory. Surrogates are used to denote the location of the token and are necessary for re-identification in free form text. In order for inspection to work properly, the name of this info type must not occur naturally anywhere in your data; otherwise, inspection may either - reverse a surrogate that does not correspond to an actual identifier - be unable to parse the surrogate and result in an error Therefore, choose your custom info type name carefully after considering what your data looks like. One way to select a name that has a high chance of yielding reliable detection is to include one or more unicode characters that are highly improbable to exist in your data. For example, assuming your data is entered from a regular ASCII keyboard, the symbol with the hex code point 29DD might be used like so: ⧝MY_TOKEN_TYPE.
---@field context? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2FieldId A context may be used for higher security and maintaining referential integrity such that the same identifier in two different contexts will be given a distinct surrogate. The context is appended to plaintext value being encrypted. On decryption the provided context is validated against the value used during encryption. If a context was provided during encryption, same context must be provided during decryption as well. If the context is not set, plaintext would be used as is for encryption. If the context is set but: 1. there is no record present when transforming a given value or 2. the field is not present when transforming a given value, plaintext would be used as is for encryption. Note that case (1) is expected when an `InfoTypeTransformation` is applied to both structured and unstructured `ContentItem`s.

---Pseudonymization method that generates surrogates via cryptographic hashing. Uses SHA-256. The key size must be either 32 or 64 bytes. Outputs a base64 encoded representation of the hashed output (for example, L7k0BHmF1ha5U3NfGykjro4xWi1MPVQPjhMAZbSV9mM=). Currently, only string and integer values can be hashed. See https://docs.cloud.google.com/sensitive-data-protection/docs/pseudonymization to learn more.
---@class gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2CryptoHashConfig
---@field cryptoKey? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2CryptoKey The key used by the hash function.

---This is a data encryption key (DEK) (as opposed to a key encryption key (KEK) stored by Cloud Key Management Service (Cloud KMS). When using Cloud KMS to wrap or unwrap a DEK, be sure to set an appropriate IAM policy on the KEK to ensure an attacker cannot unwrap the DEK.
---@class gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2CryptoKey
---@field transient? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2TransientCryptoKey Transient crypto key
---@field unwrapped? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2UnwrappedCryptoKey Unwrapped crypto key
---@field kmsWrapped? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2KmsWrappedCryptoKey Key wrapped using Cloud KMS

---Replaces an identifier with a surrogate using Format Preserving Encryption (FPE) with the FFX mode of operation; however when used in the `ReidentifyContent` API method, it serves the opposite function by reversing the surrogate back into the original identifier. The identifier must be encoded as ASCII. For a given crypto key and context, the same identifier will be replaced with the same surrogate. Identifiers must be at least two characters long. In the case that the identifier is the empty string, it will be skipped. See https://docs.cloud.google.com/sensitive-data-protection/docs/pseudonymization to learn more. Note: We recommend using CryptoDeterministicConfig for all use cases which do not require preserving the input alphabet space and size, plus warrant referential integrity. FPE incurs significant latency costs.
---@class gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2CryptoReplaceFfxFpeConfig
---@field cryptoKey? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2CryptoKey Required. The key used by the encryption algorithm.
---@field surrogateInfoType? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2InfoType The custom infoType to annotate the surrogate with. This annotation will be applied to the surrogate by prefixing it with the name of the custom infoType followed by the number of characters comprising the surrogate. The following scheme defines the format: info_type_name(surrogate_character_count):surrogate For example, if the name of custom infoType is 'MY_TOKEN_INFO_TYPE' and the surrogate is 'abc', the full replacement value will be: 'MY_TOKEN_INFO_TYPE(3):abc' This annotation identifies the surrogate when inspecting content using the custom infoType [`SurrogateType`](https://docs.cloud.google.com/sensitive-data-protection/docs/reference/rest/v2/InspectConfig#surrogatetype). This facilitates reversal of the surrogate when it occurs in free text. In order for inspection to work properly, the name of this infoType must not occur naturally anywhere in your data; otherwise, inspection may find a surrogate that does not correspond to an actual identifier. Therefore, choose your custom infoType name carefully after considering what your data looks like. One way to select a name that has a high chance of yielding reliable detection is to include one or more unicode characters that are highly improbable to exist in your data. For example, assuming your data is entered from a regular ASCII keyboard, the symbol with the hex code point 29DD might be used like so: ⧝MY_TOKEN_TYPE
---@field radix? number The native way to select the alphabet. Must be in the range [2, 95].
---@field commonAlphabet? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2CryptoReplaceFfxFpeConfigCommonAlphabetEnum|string Common alphabets.
---@field context? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2FieldId The 'tweak', a context may be used for higher security since the same identifier in two different contexts won't be given the same surrogate. If the context is not set, a default tweak will be used. If the context is set but: 1. there is no record present when transforming a given value or 1. the field is not present when transforming a given value, a default tweak will be used. Note that case (1) is expected when an `InfoTypeTransformation` is applied to both structured and unstructured `ContentItem`s. Currently, the referenced field may be of value type integer or string. The tweak is constructed as a sequence of bytes in big endian byte order such that: - a 64 bit integer is encoded followed by a single byte of value 1 - a string is encoded in UTF-8 format followed by a single byte of value 2
---@field customAlphabet? string This is supported by mapping these to the alphanumeric characters that the FFX mode natively supports. This happens before/after encryption/decryption. Each character listed must appear only once. Number of characters must be in the range [2, 95]. This must be encoded as ASCII. The order of characters does not matter. The full list of allowed characters is: ``0123456789ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz~`!@#$%^&*()_-+={[}]|\:;"'<,>.?/``

---@alias gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2CryptoReplaceFfxFpeConfigCommonAlphabetEnum "FFX_COMMON_NATIVE_ALPHABET_UNSPECIFIED"|"NUMERIC"|"HEXADECIMAL"|"UPPER_CASE_ALPHA_NUMERIC"|"ALPHA_NUMERIC"

---Custom information type provided by the user. Used to find domain-specific sensitive information configurable to the data in question.
---@class gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2CustomInfoType
---@field detectionRules? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2DetectionRuleList Set of detection rules to apply to all findings of this CustomInfoType. Rules are applied in the order that they are specified. Only supported for the `dictionary`, `regex`, and `stored_type` CustomInfoTypes.
---@field sensitivityScore? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2SensitivityScore Sensitivity for this CustomInfoType. If this CustomInfoType extends an existing InfoType, the sensitivity here will take precedence over that of the original InfoType. If unset for a CustomInfoType, it will default to HIGH. This only applies to data profiling.
---@field likelihood? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2CustomInfoTypeLikelihoodEnum|string Likelihood to return for this CustomInfoType. This base value can be altered by a detection rule if the finding meets the criteria specified by the rule. Defaults to `VERY_LIKELY` if not specified.
---@field storedType? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2StoredType Loads an existing `StoredInfoType` resource.
---@field infoType? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2InfoType CustomInfoType can either be a new infoType, or an extension of built-in infoType, when the name matches one of existing infoTypes and that infoType is specified in `InspectContent.info_types` field. Specifying the latter adds findings to the one detected by the system. If built-in info type is not specified in `InspectContent.info_types` list then the name is treated as a custom info type.
---@field exclusionType? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2CustomInfoTypeExclusionTypeEnum|string If set to EXCLUSION_TYPE_EXCLUDE this infoType will not cause a finding to be returned. It still can be used for rules matching. Only supported for the `dictionary`, `regex`, and `stored_type` CustomInfoTypes.
---@field regex? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2Regex Regular expression based CustomInfoType.
---@field surrogateType? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2SurrogateType Message for detecting output from deidentification transformations that support reversing.
---@field fileLabelInfoType? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2FileLabelInfoType File label to detect.
---@field metadataKeyValueExpression? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2MetadataKeyValueExpression Key-value pair to detect in the metadata.
---@field dictionary? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2Dictionary A list of phrases to detect as a CustomInfoType.

---@alias gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2CustomInfoTypeExclusionTypeEnum "EXCLUSION_TYPE_UNSPECIFIED"|"EXCLUSION_TYPE_EXCLUDE"

---@alias gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2CustomInfoTypeLikelihoodEnum "LIKELIHOOD_UNSPECIFIED"|"VERY_UNLIKELY"|"UNLIKELY"|"POSSIBLE"|"LIKELY"|"VERY_LIKELY"

---@alias gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2CustomInfoTypeList gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2CustomInfoType[]

---A task to execute when a data profile has been generated.
---@class gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2DataProfileAction
---@field pubSubNotification? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2PubSubNotification Publish a message into the Pub/Sub topic.
---@field tagResources? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2TagResources Tags the profiled resources with the specified tag values.
---@field publishToScc? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2PublishToSecurityCommandCenter Publishes findings to Security Command Center for each data profile.
---@field exportData? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2Export Export data profiles into a provided location.
---@field publishToDataplexCatalog? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2PublishToDataplexCatalog Publishes a portion of each profile to Dataplex Universal Catalog with the aspect type Sensitive Data Protection Profile.
---@field publishToChronicle? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2PublishToChronicle Publishes generated data profiles to Google Security Operations. For more information, see [Use Sensitive Data Protection data in context-aware analytics](https://docs.cloud.google.com/chronicle/docs/detection/usecase-dlp-high-risk-user-download).

---@alias gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2DataProfileActionList gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2DataProfileAction[]

---Snapshot of the configurations used to generate the profile.
---@class gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2DataProfileConfigSnapshot
---@field dataProfileJob? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2DataProfileJobConfig A copy of the configuration used to generate this profile. This is deprecated, and the DiscoveryConfig field is preferred moving forward. DataProfileJobConfig will still be written here for Discovery in BigQuery for backwards compatibility, but will not be updated with new fields, while DiscoveryConfig will.
---@field inspectTemplateName? string Name of the inspection template used to generate this profile
---@field inspectTemplateModifiedTime? string Timestamp when the template was modified
---@field inspectConfig? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2InspectConfig A copy of the inspection config used to generate this profile. This is a copy of the inspect_template specified in `DataProfileJobConfig`.
---@field discoveryConfig? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2DiscoveryConfig A copy of the configuration used to generate this profile.

---Configuration for setting up a job to scan resources for profile generation. Only one data profile configuration may exist per organization, folder, or project. The generated data profiles are retained according to the [data retention policy] (https://docs.cloud.google.com/sensitive-data-protection/docs/data-profiles#retention).
---@class gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2DataProfileJobConfig
---@field otherCloudStartingLocation? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2OtherCloudDiscoveryStartingLocation Must be set only when scanning other clouds.
---@field dataProfileActions? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2DataProfileActionList Actions to execute at the completion of the job.
---@field location? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2DataProfileLocation The data to scan.
---@field projectId? string The project that will run the scan. The DLP service account that exists within this project must have access to all resources that are profiled, and the DLP API must be enabled.
---@field inspectTemplates? gcp.dlp_v2.StringList Detection logic for profile generation. Not all template features are used by profiles. FindingLimits, include_quote and exclude_info_types have no impact on data profiling. Multiple templates may be provided if there is data in multiple regions. At most one template must be specified per-region (including "global"). Each region is scanned using the applicable template. If no region-specific template is specified, but a "global" template is specified, it will be copied to that region and used instead. If no global or region-specific template is provided for a region with data, that region's data will not be scanned. For more information, see https://docs.cloud.google.com/sensitive-data-protection/docs/data-profiles#data-residency.

---The data that will be profiled.
---@class gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2DataProfileLocation
---@field folderId? string The ID of the folder within an organization to scan.
---@field organizationId? string The ID of an organization to scan.

---A condition for determining whether a Pub/Sub should be triggered.
---@class gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2DataProfilePubSubCondition
---@field expressions? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2PubSubExpressions An expression.

---Score is a summary of all elements in the data profile. A higher number means more risk.
---@class gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2DataRiskLevel
---@field score? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2DataRiskLevelScoreEnum The score applied to the resource.

---@alias gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2DataRiskLevelScoreEnum "RISK_SCORE_UNSPECIFIED"|"RISK_LOW"|"RISK_UNKNOWN"|"RISK_MODERATE"|"RISK_HIGH"

---Message used to identify the type of resource being profiled.
---@class gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2DataSourceType
---@field dataSource? string A string that identifies the type of resource being profiled. Current values: * google/bigquery/table * google/project * google/sql/table * google/gcs/bucket

---Match database resources using regex filters. Examples of database resources are tables, views, and stored procedures.
---@class gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2DatabaseResourceCollection
---@field includeRegexes? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2DatabaseResourceRegexes A collection of regular expressions to match a database resource against.

---Identifies a single database resource, like a table within a database.
---@class gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2DatabaseResourceReference
---@field instance? string Required. The instance where this resource is located. For example: Cloud SQL instance ID.
---@field database? string Required. Name of a database within the instance.
---@field projectId? string Required. If within a project-level config, then this must match the config's project ID.
---@field databaseResource? string Required. Name of a database resource, for example, a table within the database.

---A pattern to match against one or more database resources. At least one pattern must be specified. Regular expressions use RE2 [syntax](https://github.com/google/re2/wiki/Syntax); a guide can be found under the google/re2 repository on GitHub.
---@class gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2DatabaseResourceRegex
---@field instanceRegex? string Regex to test the instance name against. If empty, all instances match.
---@field projectIdRegex? string For organizations, if unset, will match all projects. Has no effect for configurations created within a project.
---@field databaseRegex? string Regex to test the database name against. If empty, all databases match.
---@field databaseResourceNameRegex? string Regex to test the database resource's name against. An example of a database resource name is a table's name. Other database resource names like view names could be included in the future. If empty, all database resources match.

---@alias gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2DatabaseResourceRegexList gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2DatabaseResourceRegex[]

---A collection of regular expressions to determine what database resources to match against.
---@class gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2DatabaseResourceRegexes
---@field patterns? gcp.dlp_v2.GooglePrivacyDlpV2DatabaseResourceRegexList A group of regular expression patterns to match against one or more database resources. Maximum of 100 entries. The sum of all regular expression's length can't exceed 10 KiB.

